  <exercisegroup>
    <introduction>
      <p>In the following exercises, estimate the areas under the curves by computing the left Riemann sums, <m>L_8</m>.</p>
    </introduction>

    <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572330294">
      <statement>
        <image source="ch5/CNX_Calc_Figure_05_01_201.jpg" width="50%">
          <description>A graph of a function that increases linearly with a slope of 1 from (0, 1) to (3, 4). It curves from (3, 4) to (5, 4), changing direction from increasing to decreasing at (4, 5). Finally, it decreases linearly with a slope of -1 from (5, 4) to (8, 1).</description>
        </image>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572129114">
      <statement>
        <image source="ch5/CNX_Calc_Figure_05_01_202.jpg" width="50%">
          <description>The graph of a smooth curve going through the points (0, 3), (1, 2), (2, 1), (3, 2), (4, 3), (5, 4), (6, 5), (7, 4), and (8, 3).</description>
        </image>
      </statement>
      <solution xml:id="fs-id1170572129130">
        <p><m>L_8 = 3 + 2 + 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 + 4 = 24</m></p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572223504">
      <statement>
        <image source="ch5/CNX_Calc_Figure_05_01_203.jpg" width="50%">
          <description>The graph of a smooth curve going through the points (0, 0), (1, 1), (2, 2), (3, 1), (4, 3), (5, 2), (6, 4), (7, 5), and (8, 7).</description>
        </image>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572223574">
      <statement>
        <image source="ch5/CNX_Calc_Figure_05_01_204.jpg" width="50%">
          <description>The graph of a smooth curve going through the points (0, 3), (1, 5), (2, 7), (3, 6), (4, 8), (5, 6), (6, 5), (7, 4), and (8, 6).</description>
        </image>
      </statement>
      <solution xml:id="fs-id1170572309712">
        <p><m>L_8 = 3 + 5 + 7 + 6 + 8 + 6 + 5 + 4 = 44</m></p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </exercisegroup>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572309766">
    <statement>
      <p><term>[T]</term> Use a computer algebra system to compute the Riemann sum, <m>L_N</m>, for <m>N = 10, 30, 50</m> for <m>f(x) = \sqrt{1 - x^2}</m> on <m>[-1, 1]</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170571547452">
    <statement>
      <p><term>[T]</term> Use a computer algebra system to compute the Riemann sum, <m>L_N</m>, for <m>N = 10, 30, 50</m> for <m>f(x) = \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{1 + x^2}}</m> on <m>[-1, 1]</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution xml:id="fs-id1170571628957">
      <p><m>L_{10} \approx 1.7604</m>, <m>L_{30} \approx 1.7625</m>, <m>L_{50} \approx 1.76265</m></p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170571629004">
    <statement>
      <p><term>[T]</term> Use a computer algebra system to compute the Riemann sum, <m>L_N</m>, for <m>N = 10, 30, 50</m> for <m>f(x) = \sin^2 x</m> on <m>[0, 2\pi]</m>. Compare these estimates with <m>\pi</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercisegroup>
    <introduction>
      <p>In the following exercises, use a calculator or a computer program to evaluate the endpoint sums <m>R_N</m> and <m>L_N</m> for <m>N = 1, 10, 100</m>. How do these estimates compare with the exact answers, which you can find via geometry?</p>
    </introduction>

    <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572351542">
      <statement>
        <p><term>[T]</term> <m>y = \cos(\pi x)</m> on the interval <m>[0, 1]</m></p>
      </statement>
      <solution xml:id="fs-id1170572351587">
        <p><m>R_1 = -1</m>, <m>L_1 = 1</m>, <m>R_{10} = -0.1</m>, <m>L_{10} = 0.1</m>, <m>L_{100} = 0.01</m>, and <m>R_{100} = -0.01</m>. By symmetry of the graph, the exact net signed area is zero.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170571638124">
      <statement>
        <p><term>[T]</term> <m>y = 3x + 2</m> on the interval <m>[3, 5]</m></p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercisegroup>

  <exercisegroup>
    <introduction>
      <p>In the following exercises, use a calculator or a computer program to evaluate the endpoint sums <m>R_N</m> and <m>L_N</m> for <m>N = 1, 10, 100</m>.</p>
    </introduction>

    <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572448428">
      <statement>
        <p><term>[T]</term> <m>y = x^4 - 5x^2 + 4</m> on the interval <m>[-2, 2]</m>, which has an exact area of <m>\dfrac{32}{15}</m></p>
      </statement>
      <solution xml:id="fs-id1170572448493">
        <p><m>R_1 = 0</m>, <m>L_1 = 0</m>, <m>R_{10} = 2.4499</m>, <m>L_{10} = 2.4499</m>, <m>R_{100} = 2.1365</m>, <m>L_{100} = 2.1365</m></p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572368499">
      <statement>
        <p><term>[T]</term> <m>y = \ln x</m> on the interval <m>[1, 2]</m>, which has an exact area of <m>2\ln(2) - 1</m></p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercisegroup>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572306445">
    <statement>
      <p>Explain why, if <m>f(a) \geq 0</m> and <m>f</m> is increasing on <m>[a, b]</m>, the left-endpoint estimate is a lower bound for the area below the graph of <m>f</m> on <m>[a, b]</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution xml:id="fs-id1170572503292">
      <p>If <m>[c, d]</m> is a subinterval of <m>[a, b]</m> under one of the left-endpoint sum rectangles, then the area of the rectangle contributing to the left-endpoint estimate is <m>f(c)(d - c)</m>. Because <m>f(c) \leq f(x)</m> for <m>c \leq x \leq d</m>, the area under the graph of <m>f</m> between <m>c</m> and <m>d</m> is <m>f(c)(d - c)</m> plus the area below the graph of <m>f</m> but above the horizontal line segment at height <m>f(c)</m>, which is nonnegative. As this is true for each left-endpoint sum interval, it follows that the left Riemann sum is less than or equal to the area below the graph of <m>f</m> on <m>[a, b]</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170571813937">
    <statement>
      <p>Explain why, if <m>f(b) \geq 0</m> and <m>f</m> is decreasing on <m>[a, b]</m>, the left-endpoint estimate is an upper bound for the area below the graph of <m>f</m> on <m>[a, b]</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572480435">
    <statement>
      <p>Show that, in general, <m>R_N - L_N = (b - a) \times \dfrac{f(b) - f(a)}{N}</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution xml:id="fs-id1170572624704">
      <p><m>\displaystyle L_N = \frac{b - a}{N}\sum_{i=1}^N f\left(a + (b - a)\frac{i - 1}{N}\right) = \frac{b - a}{N}\sum_{i=0}^{N-1} f\left(a + (b - a)\frac{i}{N}\right)</m> and <m>\displaystyle R_N = \frac{b - a}{N}\sum_{i=1}^N f\left(a + (b - a)\frac{i}{N}\right)</m>. The left sum has a term corresponding to <m>i = 0</m> and the right sum has a term corresponding to <m>i = N</m>. In <m>R_N - L_N</m>, any term corresponding to <m>i = 1, 2, \ldots, N - 1</m> occurs once with a plus sign and once with a minus sign, so each such term cancels and one is left with <m>R_N - L_N = \dfrac{b - a}{N}\bigl(f(b) - f(a)\bigr)</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170571629738">
    <statement>
      <p>Explain why, if <m>f</m> is increasing on <m>[a, b]</m>, the error between either <m>L_N</m> or <m>R_N</m> and the area <m>A</m> below the graph of <m>f</m> is at most <m>(b - a)\dfrac{f(b) - f(a)}{N}</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170571624171">
    <introduction>
      <p>For each of the three graphs:</p>
      <image source="ch5/CNX_Calc_Figure_05_01_212.jpg" width="50%">
        <description>Three graphs, stacked vertically, drawn on graph paper. Each shows the same image. However, the axes become progressively more exact in units. The first is marked in units, from negative 3 units to positive 3 units on each axis. The second has the half-units marked, and the third has the quarter units marked. As such, the graph paper boxes become smaller and smaller. The image is symmetrical across each axis and is a curved cross shape. It meets the axes at (0, 3), (3, 0), (0, -3), and (-3, 0) and has corners roughly at (0.7, 0.7), (0.7, -0.7), (-0.7, -0.7), and (-0.7, 0.7). In graph 1, no square unit boxes are completely contained inside the shape. Twenty boxes are enclosed partially by the shape. In graph 2, nine boxes are completely contained inside the shape, and eleven boxes are enclosed partially by the shape. In graph 3, 11 boxes are completely contained inside the shape, and 4.5 are enclosed partially by the shape.</description>
      </image>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Obtain a lower bound <m>L(A)</m> for the area enclosed by the curve by adding the areas of the squares <em>enclosed completely</em> by the curve.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>Graph 1: <m>L(A) = 0</m>. Graph 2: <m>L(A) = 9</m>. Graph 3: <m>L(A) = 11.0</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Obtain an upper bound <m>U(A)</m> for the area by adding to <m>L(A)</m> the areas <m>B(A)</m> of the squares <em>enclosed partially</em> by the curve.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>Graph 1: <m>B(A) = 20</m>, <m>U(A) = 20</m>. Graph 2: <m>B(A) = 11</m>, <m>U(A) = 20</m>. Graph 3: <m>B(A) = 4.5</m>, <m>U(A) = 15.5</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572617950">
    <statement>
      <p>In the previous exercise, explain why <m>L(A)</m> gets no smaller while <m>U(A)</m> gets no larger as the squares are subdivided into four boxes of equal area.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="fs-id1170572617995">
    <statement>
      <p>A unit circle is made up of <m>n</m> wedges equivalent to the inner wedge in the figure. The base of the inner triangle is <m>2\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{n}\right)</m> and its height is <m>\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{n}\right)</m> (equivalently, its area is <m>\frac{1}{2}\sin\left(\frac{2\pi}{n}\right)</m>). The base of the outer triangle is <m>B = 2\tan\left(\frac{\pi}{n}\right)</m> and the height is <m>H = 1</m> (or with slant side <m>1</m>, base <m>B = \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{n}\right) + \sin\left(\frac{\pi}{n}\right)\tan\left(\frac{\pi}{n}\right)</m> and height <m>H = B\sin\left(\frac{2\pi}{n}\right)</m>). Use this information to argue that the area of a unit circle is equal to <m>\pi</m>.</p>
      <image source="ch5/CNX_Calc_Figure_05_01_213.jpg" width="50%">
        <description>A wedge of a circle cut at an acute angle theta = 2pi / n. Several extra lines are drawn. The first is a line A connecting the ends of the two radii, creating a triangle. The second is another line B parallel to A, tangent to the circle and intersecting the extended radii. A concentric curve C connects the endpoints of the radii, and the area between this curve and the outer tangent segment is shaded in pink, while the inner triangle is purple.</description>
      </image>
    </statement>
    <solution xml:id="fs-id1170572554298">
      <p>Let <m>A</m> be the area of the unit circle. The circle encloses <m>n</m> congruent triangles each of area <m>\dfrac{\sin\left(\frac{2\pi}{n}\right)}{2}</m>, so <m>\dfrac{n}{2}\sin\left(\frac{2\pi}{n}\right) \leq A</m>. Similarly, the circle is contained inside <m>n</m> congruent triangles each of area <m>\dfrac{BH}{2} = \dfrac{1}{2}\left(\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{n}\right) + \sin\left(\frac{\pi}{n}\right)\tan\left(\frac{\pi}{n}\right)\right)\sin\left(\frac{2\pi}{n}\right)</m>, so <m>A \leq \dfrac{n}{2}\sin\left(\frac{2\pi}{n}\right)\left(\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{n}\right) + \sin\left(\frac{\pi}{n}\right)\tan\left(\frac{\pi}{n}\right)\right)</m>. As <m>n \to \infty</m>, <m>\dfrac{n}{2}\sin\left(\frac{2\pi}{n}\right) = \dfrac{\pi\sin\left(\frac{2\pi}{n}\right)}{\frac{2\pi}{n}} \to \pi</m>, so we conclude <m>\pi \leq A</m>. Also, as <m>n \to \infty</m>, <m>\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{n}\right) + \sin\left(\frac{\pi}{n}\right)\tan\left(\frac{\pi}{n}\right) \to 1</m>, so we also have <m>A \leq \pi</m>. By the squeeze theorem for limits, we conclude that <m>A = \pi</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

</exercises>

</section>